    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two-dimensional hexagonal Boron Nitride (2D hBN) is a uniquely rich 2D material due to its interesting opto-electronic properties. hBN’s large bandgap makes it an insulator, but also lends it interesting optical deep ultra-violet (DUV) properties that remain largely unexplored. A better understanding of these optical properties could provide us information about the modified band structure of mono- and few-layer hBN, as well as twisted, stacked hBN. In particular, hBN with a small twist angle close to 0 exhibits ferroelectricity. In this report, I explore the optical properties of twisted and ferroelectric hBN stacks on SiO2/Si substrates. Four main contributions were made: (1) The mechanical exfoliation of large, thin hBN flakes was significantly improved by using a micromanipulator during the transfer of hBN to the substrate. (2) A transfer matrix method model was programmed to rapidly estimate the thickness of few-layer hBN flakes based solely on their reflectance spectra, and was adapted to fit twisted hBN stacks with non-linear least squares optimization. (3) The amplitude of the interference patterns in reflectance spectra of annealed hBN stacks was shown to decrease with increased angle. (4) The different stacking sequence of ferroelectric and non-ferroelectric hBN stacks exhibit different optical signatures and excitonic features, but further research is required to better understand the relationship between ferroelectric stacking order, individual ferroelectric domains, excitons, and optical measurements.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
